 questions:mix([
  q(MEI,'During which phase of the cell cycle does the cell grow and make proteins before DNA is copied?','G1 phase',['S phase','G2 phase','M phase, when the nucleus divides into two'],'A','G1 is the first gap phase, when the cell grows, makes organelles and checks conditions before S phase.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'Which part of the cell cycle takes up most of a typical cell’s life?','Interphase',['Prophase','Metaphase','Cytokinesis, the splitting of the cytoplasm'],'B','Interphase includes G1, S and G2, and it lasts far longer than the steps of mitosis.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'In animal cells, which structure organizes the spindle microtubules during mitosis?','The centrosome',['The nucleolus','The cell plate','The Golgi apparatus, which packages proteins'],'C','Each centrosome has a pair of centrioles, and the two centrosomes move to opposite poles.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'Which protein complex holds sister chromatids together until anaphase?','Cohesin',['Kinesin','Actin','Tubulin, which builds the spindle fibers'],'D','Cohesin is cut by an enzyme at the start of anaphase, which lets sister chromatids separate.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'Where in the human male does spermatogenesis take place?','In the seminiferous tubules of the testes',['In the prostate gland','In the vas deferens','In the urethra'],'A','Sperm form in the seminiferous tubules and then mature in the epididymis.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'After meiosis I is complete, the two daughter cells are best described as:','Haploid, with each chromosome still made of two chromatids',['Diploid with single chromatids','Identical to the parent cell','Haploid with single unreplicated chromatids'],'B','Meiosis I separates homologs, which halves the chromosome number; meiosis II then separates sister chromatids.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'In plants with alternation of generations, which stage is haploid and makes gametes by mitosis?','Gametophyte',['Sporophyte','Zygote','Seed coat, which protects the embryo'],'C','The gametophyte is haploid, so it can make gametes by mitosis rather than meiosis.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'In human oogenesis, when is meiosis II completed?','Only if the egg is fertilized by a sperm',['Before birth','At puberty','At the start of each menstrual cycle, before ovulation'],'D','The secondary oocyte is paused in metaphase II at ovulation and finishes meiosis only after fertilization.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'A flatworm splits into pieces and each piece grows into a whole worm. What type of reproduction is this?','Fragmentation',['Budding','Binary fission','Sexual reproduction with two parents'],'A','Fragmentation is asexual, so each new worm is genetically identical to the original.',CAR),
  q(MEI,'How does an oncogene differ from a normal proto-oncogene?','It is a mutated form that pushes the cell to divide too much',['It stops the cell cycle','It repairs damaged DNA','It is a tumor suppressor gene that triggers programmed cell death'],'B','Proto-oncogenes normally promote division in a controlled way; a gain-of-function mutation turns them into oncogenes.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'What is the probability that a couple’s next three children will all be boys?','1/8',['1/2','1/4','3/8, the chance of exactly two boys'],'C','Each birth is an independent event, so multiply the chances: (1/2) × (1/2) × (1/2) = 1/8.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'A pure-breeding tall plant (TT) is crossed with a short plant (tt). What genotypes appear in the F1?','All Tt',['All TT','Half TT and half tt','1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, as in an F2'],'D','Each parent can give only one kind of allele, so every F1 plant is heterozygous.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'Two Aa parents have a child who shows the dominant phenotype. What is the chance the child is a carrier (Aa)?','2/3',['1/2','1/4','3/4, the chance of any dominant phenotype'],'A','Among the dominant offspring (AA, Aa, Aa), two of the three are heterozygous.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'In mice, one gene controls black or brown color and another controls whether any pigment is made. Which dihybrid ratio is expected?','9 : 3 : 4',['9 : 3 : 3 : 1','12 : 3 : 1','15 : 1'],'B','This is recessive epistasis: the albino genotype hides the color gene, so two classes merge into one group of 4.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'A dihybrid cross gives 15 plants with the dominant trait for every 1 plant with the recessive trait. What does this suggest?','Two duplicate genes, where a dominant allele of either gene gives the trait',['Codominance','Sex linkage','One gene with incomplete dominance that blends the two parental traits'],'C','Only aabb plants lack the trait, so the 9 : 3 : 3 classes merge into 15 versus 1.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'In the cross AaBb × AaBb, what fraction of offspring will be aabb?','1/16',['1/4','1/8','9/16'],'D','The chance of aa is 1/4 and of bb is 1/4, so the chance of both is 1/16.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'A woman is affected by an X-linked recessive disorder. Which of her sons will be affected?','All of them',['None of them','Half of them','Only the sons whose father also carries the same recessive allele'],'A','An affected woman has two recessive X alleles, so every son receives one and has no second X to mask it.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'Two carriers (Aa × Aa) have three children. What is the probability that exactly one child is affected?','27/64',['1/4','9/64','1/64, the chance that all three are affected'],'B','There are 3 birth orders, each with chance (1/4)(3/4)(3/4) = 9/64, so the total is 27/64.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'What genotypic ratio results from a cross of Aa × Aa?','1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa',['3 : 1','1 : 1','9 : 3 : 3 : 1'],'C','The 3 : 1 ratio describes phenotypes, while genotypes split as 1 : 2 : 1.',CAR),
  q(CLAS,'In Huntington disease, symptoms often appear earlier and more severely in each generation. What is this called?','Anticipation',['Penetrance','Pleiotropy','Epistasis'],'D','Anticipation in Huntington disease is linked to the CAG repeat growing longer when it is passed on.',CAR),
  q(CHR,'A female with the karyotype 47,XXX has which condition?','Triple X syndrome',['Turner syndrome','Klinefelter syndrome','Down syndrome'],'A','Triple X often causes few symptoms, because extra X chromosomes are inactivated as Barr bodies.',CAR),
  q(CHR,'A deletion on chromosome 15 inherited from the father causes which syndrome?','Prader-Willi syndrome',['Angelman syndrome','Cri du chat syndrome','Turner syndrome, which involves the X chromosome'],'B','The same deletion inherited from the mother causes Angelman syndrome, an effect of genomic imprinting.',CAR),
  q(CHR,'What is aneuploidy?','Having an abnormal number of one or more chromosomes',['Having three full sets of chromosomes','A segment flipped within a chromosome','Having a normal number of chromosomes'],'C','Trisomies and monosomies are both forms of aneuploidy, usually caused by nondisjunction.',CAR),
  q(CHR,'The Philadelphia chromosome, linked to chronic myeloid leukemia, results from which change?','A translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22',['A deletion on chromosome 5','Trisomy 21','A duplication of the entire X chromosome in every cell'],'D','The translocation joins two genes into one, making a protein that drives uncontrolled blood cell growth.',CAR),
  q(CHR,'A person has some cells with 46 chromosomes and others with 47. What is this called?','Mosaicism',['Polyploidy','Monosomy','Translocation'],'A','Mosaicism happens when nondisjunction occurs during mitosis after fertilization, giving two cell lines.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Thomas Hunt Morgan’s work with white-eyed fruit flies provided evidence that:','Genes are located on chromosomes',['DNA is double-stranded','Proteins carry genetic information','Traits blend together in each generation'],'B','Morgan found white eyes were linked to the X chromosome, supporting the chromosome theory of inheritance.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Avery, MacLeod and McCarty showed that the “transforming principle” in Griffith’s experiment was:','DNA',['Protein','RNA','A lipid in the bacterial capsule'],'C','When enzymes destroyed DNA, transformation stopped, but destroying protein or RNA did not stop it.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'A chi-square value is smaller than the critical value. What should the researcher conclude?','Fail to reject the null hypothesis; differences may be due to chance',['Reject the null hypothesis','The experiment must be wrong','The genes must be linked on one chromosome'],'D','A small chi-square means the observed counts fit the expected ratio closely enough.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Which is an example of quantitative data in a genetics experiment?','The number of offspring with red eyes',['The color of the eyes','The shape of the wings','Whether flies look healthy'],'A','Quantitative data are measured or counted, while qualitative data describe qualities like color.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Whose X-ray diffraction images helped reveal the double-helix structure of DNA?','Rosalind Franklin',['Gregor Mendel','Barbara McClintock','Frederick Griffith, who studied bacterial transformation'],'B','Franklin’s Photo 51 showed a helical pattern that Watson and Crick used in their model.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Which graph is best for showing how a fruit fly population changes over several weeks?','A line graph',['A pie chart','A bar graph of one week only','A table with no units'],'C','Line graphs show change in a continuous variable, such as time, on the x-axis.',CAR),
  q(EXP,'Why do geneticists start crosses with true-breeding (pure-breeding) parents?','Their genotypes are known and they produce consistent offspring',['They have more offspring','They never mutate','They carry both alleles for every trait, so all phenotypes appear'],'D','True-breeding lines are homozygous, so the results of the cross can be predicted and interpreted.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Which nitrogen bases are purines with a double-ring structure?','Adenine and guanine',['Cytosine and thymine','Cytosine and uracil','Thymine and uracil'],'A','A purine always pairs with a pyrimidine, which keeps the width of the DNA helix constant.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Which base is found in RNA but not in DNA?','Uracil',['Thymine','Adenine','Guanine'],'B','In RNA uracil pairs with adenine, taking the place of thymine.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Which enzyme joins Okazaki fragments into one continuous strand?','DNA ligase',['Helicase','Primase','RNA polymerase'],'C','DNA ligase forms the final bond in the sugar-phosphate backbone after primers are replaced.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'What is added to the 5′ end of eukaryotic mRNA to protect it and help ribosomes bind?','A modified guanine cap',['A poly-A tail','An intron','A stop codon'],'D','The 5′ cap protects mRNA from breakdown and helps start translation.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Most amino acids are coded by more than one codon. What is this property called?','Redundancy (degeneracy) of the genetic code',['Ambiguity','Frameshifting','Wobble pairing at the first base'],'A','Redundancy means some point mutations do not change the amino acid, giving silent mutations.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Which enzyme relieves the twisting strain ahead of the replication fork?','Topoisomerase',['Ligase','Primase','Telomerase'],'B','Topoisomerase cuts and rejoins DNA strands so the helix does not become overwound as it unwinds.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Which DNA region can increase transcription of a gene even when it is far away from the gene?','Enhancer',['Promoter','Terminator','Start codon'],'C','Activator proteins bind enhancers and loop the DNA to help RNA polymerase at the promoter.',CAR),
  q(MOL,'Barbara McClintock discovered DNA segments that can move to new positions. What are they called?','Transposons',['Plasmids','Operons','Telomeres'],'D','Transposons, or “jumping genes,” can disrupt genes when they insert, as seen in the colors of corn kernels.',CAR),
  q(POP,'In a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, q = 0.3. What is the frequency of the homozygous recessive genotype?','0.09',['0.3','0.42','0.49, the frequency of the dominant homozygotes'],'A','The homozygous recessive frequency is q², and 0.3 × 0.3 = 0.09.',CAR),
  q(POP,'In evolution, what does biological fitness measure?','How many offspring an organism leaves that survive to reproduce',['How strong the organism is','How long the organism lives','How long an organism survives compared with others in its population'],'B','Fitness is reproductive success, not physical strength or lifespan.',CAR),
  q(POP,'A new plant species forms in the same area as its parent species after a polyploidy event. What is this?','Sympatric speciation',['Allopatric speciation','Gene flow','Genetic drift'],'C','Polyploid plants often cannot breed with the parent species, so they become isolated without any barrier.',CAR),
  q(POP,'Individuals tend to choose mates that look like themselves. What is this called?','Assortative mating',['Random mating','Gene flow','Natural selection on survival only'],'D','Assortative mating is nonrandom and increases homozygous genotypes for the trait involved.',CAR),
  q(POP,'Breeding dogs for certain traits over many generations is an example of:','Artificial selection',['Natural selection','Genetic drift','Founder effect'],'A','In artificial selection, humans choose which individuals breed, so trait frequencies change quickly.',CAR),
  q(MOD,'Which technique detects a specific DNA sequence after gel electrophoresis using a labeled probe?','Southern blot',['Northern blot','Western blot','ELISA'],'B','A Southern blot detects DNA, a Northern blot detects RNA, and a Western blot detects proteins.',CAR),
  q(MOD,'Which tool measures the expression of thousands of genes at once?','A DNA microarray',['A karyotype','A Punnett square','A pedigree chart'],'C','Microarrays compare gene activity, for example between cancer cells and normal cells.',CAR),
  q(MOD,'Dolly the sheep was cloned using which technique?','Somatic cell nuclear transfer',['PCR','Gel electrophoresis','In vitro fertilization with sperm and egg'],'D','The nucleus of a body cell was placed into an egg whose own nucleus had been removed.',CAR),
  q(MOD,'In Sanger sequencing, what stops the growth of each DNA strand?','Dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs)',['Ligase','Primers','Restriction enzymes'],'A','A ddNTP lacks the 3′ OH group, so no more nucleotides can be added after it.',CAR),
  q(MOD,'A couple is tested before pregnancy to see if both have one copy of the cystic fibrosis allele. What is this called?','Carrier screening',['Newborn screening','Gene therapy','Forensic testing'],'B','Carrier screening shows the couple’s chance of having a child with a recessive condition.',CAR)
 ],9526)
